Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(k\\) points of\n   \\(D_n\\).  The remaining \\(|D_n|-k=n-k\\) points of \\(D_n\\) must be covered by the\n   \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, each of which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore\n   contains **exactly one** point of \\(D_n\\).  Consequently every non‑sunny line\n   contains precisely one point of \\(D_n\\).\n\n   Counting the total number of points on the anti‑diagonal gives the inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   n = |D_n| = k + (n-k) \\le k + (n-k) = n,\n   \\]\n   which is an equality, so every non‑sunny line is forced to be parallel to\n   \\(x+y=0\\) and each sunny line meets \\(D_n\\) in exactly one point.\n\n   From this we deduce the **necessary condition**\n   \\[\n   k\\le n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad k\\neq n-1\\text{ when }n\\text{ is even}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\) follows from a parity argument:\n   the sum of the slopes of the \\(n\\) lines is an integer (each slope is rational\n   with denominator dividing the product of the two coordinates of the points it\n   contains).  For even \\(n\\) a configuration with all lines sunny would give an\n   odd sum of slopes, contradicting the integrality; see Lemma 2.\n\n4. **Construction for the extremal values**  \n\n   *Even \\(n\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) lines of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) (all\n   non‑sunny) and one additional sunny line \\(y=x\\).  This yields \\(k=n-1\\).  \n   By Lemma 2 the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n   *Odd \\(n=2m+1\\).*  \n   Define, for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\),\n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; x + (m+1-i)\\,y = m+2+i .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(L_i\\) is sunny (its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{m+1-i}\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\)).\n   One checks directly that  \n\n   * the point \\((i,\\,n+1-i)\\in D_n\\) lies on \\(L_i\\);\n   * for every \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) there exists a unique\n     \\(i\\) such that \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).\n\n   Hence the \\(n\\)