this line by\nthe sunny line \\(y=x+(t-1)\\); this new line contains exactly the same points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) as the removed vertical line (namely \\((t,1),\\dots ,(t,n+1-t)\\))\nand adds one more sunny line.  The other lines are unchanged, so the total\nnumber of lines stays \\(n\\) and the number of sunny lines increases by one.\nRepeating the operation we obtain configurations for all\n\\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A necessary upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Claim 1.*  Every sunny line meets the anti‑diagonal \\(D_n\\) in **exactly one**\npoint.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a sunny line contained two distinct points of \\(D_n\\), those two points would\nhave the same sum of coordinates, hence the line would be parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.  Thus a sunny line meets\n\\(D_n\\) in at most one point.  Since the \\(n\\) lines together cover all points of\n\\(D_n\\) and there are only \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, at least \\(k\\) points of\n\\(D_n\\) must be covered by sunny lines, so each sunny line actually meets\n\\(D_n\\) in exactly one point. ∎\n\n\n\n*Claim 2.*  Every non‑sunny line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and contains exactly\none point of \\(D_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\nIf it were horizontal or vertical, it would contain at most one point of\n\\(D_n\\) (because points of \\(D_n\\) have distinct \\(x\\)‑ and \\(y\\)‑coordinates).\nBut a horizontal or vertical line would then miss many points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that lie on the same anti‑diagonal, contradicting the fact that\nall points are covered.  Hence each non‑sunny line must be parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\).  Such a line intersects the anti‑diagonal in exactly one point,\nso the claim follows. ∎\n\n\n\nFrom Claims 1 and 2 we obtain the **necessary condition**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n\\quad\\text{an