\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Realising all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\text{For every }t\\;(0\\le t\\le n-2) \\text{ there exists a configuration with exactly }t\\text{ sunny lines.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBegin with the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n),\n\\]\n\nwhich are all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,t\\) replace the line \\(V_i\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=x+i-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(+1\\) (hence sunny) and passes through the two points\n\\((i,i)\\) and \\((i+1,i+1)\\) of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  All other points of the column\n\\(x=i\\) are \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq i\\); they are already covered by the line\n\\(S_{i-1}\\) (when \\(i>1\\)) or by the unchanged vertical line \\(V_{i+1}\\) (when\n\\(i=1\\)).  Consequently the set of \\(n\\) lines after the replacements still\ncovers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Exactly \\(t\\) of them are sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Realising \\(k=n-1\\) for every \\(n\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ there exists a configuration with exactly }n-1\\text{ sunny lines.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nD_j:\\;x+y=j+1\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,n-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich are all parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non‑sunny) and together contain all\npoints of the anti‑diagonal \\(D_n\\).  Add the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) either lies on its own anti‑diagonal line\n\\(D_j\\) (if its coordinates are not equal) or lies on \\(S\\) (if the coordinates\nare equal).  Thus the \\(n\\) lines cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and exactly one of them\nis sunny; consequently we have a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 3.}\\qquad\n\\text{In any admissible configuration the number }k\\text{ of sunny lines satisfies }k\\le n,\n\\]\nand if \\(n\\) is even then \\(k\\neq n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the anti‑diagonal set \\(D_n\\) (size \\(n\\)).  \nA sunny line cannot be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it can meet \\(D_n\\) in at\nmost one point.  Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points\nof \\(D_n\\).\n\nA non‑sunny line that is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) would be horizontal or\nvertical.  Such a line meets \\(D_n\\) in at most one point, but then it could not\ncover the many other points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that lie on the same anti‑diagonal,\nwhich is impossible because all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be covered.\nConsequently every non‑sunny line must be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); each of those\nlines meets \\(D_n\\) in exactly one point.  There are \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines,\nso they cover exactly \\(n-k\\) points of \\(D_n\\).\n\nSince the \\(n\\) points of \\(D_n\\) are all covered, we have  \n\n\\[\nn = k + (n-k),\n\\]\n\nwhich forces every sunny line to meet \\(D_n\\) in exactly one point and every\nnon‑sunny line to be of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nIf \\(n\\) is even, the \\(n\\) points of \\(D_n\\) are paired into \\(\\frac n2\\) opposite\npairs \\(\\{(i,n+1-i),(n+1-i,i)\\}\\).  Two opposite points cannot lie on the same\nnon‑sunny line (they would give a line of slope \\(-1\\) again), so each opposite\npair must be split between a sunny line and a non‑sunny line.  Hence the number\nof sunny lines cannot be \\(n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 4.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }n\\text{ is even, no configuration with }k=n\\text{ exists.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  By Lemma 3 each line meets the anti‑diagonal\n\\(D_n\\) in exactly one point, so the set of slopes \\(\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_n\\}\\) is a\nbijection between the lines and the points of \\(D_n\\).\n\nFor a line through two points \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) the slope is  \n\n\\[\ns=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1},\n\\]\n\nwhere the numerator and denominator are integers whose absolute values are at most\n\\(n-1\\).  Because \\(n\\) is even, \\(n-1\\) is odd; therefore each denominator is an\nodd integer.  Consequently every slope \\(s_i\\) can be written in lowest terms as\n\\(\\frac{p_i}{q_i}\\) with odd \\(q_i\\).\n\nThe product of all slopes equals the product of the slopes of the \\(n\\) lines\nthrough the points of \\(D_n\\).  Two opposite points of \\(D_n\\) have coordinates\n\\((i,n+1-i)\\) and \\((n+1-i,i)\\); the line through them has slope \\(-1\\).  Hence the\nproduct of the \\(n\\) slopes is \\((-1)^{n}=1\\).\n\nWriting the product in lowest terms we obtain  \n\n\\[\n1=\\prod_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{p_i}{q_i}=\\frac{\\prod p_i}{\\prod q_i}.\n\\]\n\nAll \\(q_i\\) are odd, so \\(\\prod q_i\\) is odd.  Therefore the denominator of the\nfraction on the right‑hand side is odd, which forces \\(\\prod q_i=1\\).  Hence every\n\\(q_i=1\\) and each slope \\(s_i\\) is an integer.\n\nBut a sunny line cannot have integer slope \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\); the only possible\ninteger slopes are \\(\\pm2,\\pm3,\\dots\\).  The product of \\(n\\) such integers cannot be\n\\(1\\) (its absolute value would be at least \\(2^n>1\\)).  This contradiction shows\nthat a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist when \\(n\\) is even. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nA direct enumeration (or a short computer check) shows that the six points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_3\\) cannot be covered by three lines with exactly two of them sunny.\nThus for \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible.  (The argument of Lemma 4 does\nnot apply because \\(n-1=2\\) is not odd.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 7.  Existence of \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 5.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }n=2m+1\\ge5\\text{ (odd), the }n\\text{ lines }\nL_i:\\;x+(m+1-i)y=m+2+i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\nare all sunny and together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{\\,m+1-i\\,}\\); since \\(m+1-i\\) ranges over the\nnon‑zero integers \\(-m,\\dots ,m\\), the slope is never \\(0,-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\), so\neach \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nFor the anti‑diagonal point \\((i,n+1-i)=(i,2m+2-i)\\) we have  \n\n\\[\ni+(m+1-i)(2m+2-i)=i+(m+1-i)(2m+2-i)=m+2+i,\n\\]\n\nso \\((i,n+1-i)\\in L_i\\); thus each line meets \\(D_n\\) in exactly one point.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  Set \\(i=a\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) we have\n\\(b\\le 2m+2-a\\).  Substituting \\(i=a\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na+(m+1-a)b=m+2+a\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nb=\\frac{m+2}{\\,m+1-a\\,}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b\\) are positive integers and \\(a+b\\le2m+2\\), the right‑hand side is an\ninteger and equals the given \\(b\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of\n\\(L_a\\).  Therefore every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on exactly one of the\nlines \\(L_i\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 8.  Completion of the proof  \n\n*Existence.*  \nLemma 1 gives all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\); Lemma 2 gives \\(k=n-1\\); Lemma 5\ngives \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n*Non‑existence.*  \nLemma 3 shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines and\nexcludes \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even.  Lemma 4 rules out \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\);\nthe separate check for \\(n=3\\) rules out \\(k=n\\) for the smallest odd case.\nThus no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\nConsequently the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly those described in the\nstatement of the theorem. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\{\\,n-1\\,\\}\\ \\cup\\ \n\\begin{cases}\n\\{\\,n\\,\\}, & n\\ge 5\\text{ and }n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\varnothing, & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
