(1\\le i\\le k\\) replace \\(V_i\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=x-i+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny) and passes through the two points \\((i,i)\\) and \\((i+1,i+1)\\).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a\\le k\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\); points with \\(a>k\\) lie on the unchanged vertical line \\(V_a\\).  Thus all points are covered and exactly \\(k\\) lines are sunny.\n\n#### Lemma 4 (realising \\(k=n-1\\) for all \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;x=1\\qquad\\text{(non‑sunny)},\n\\]\n\nand for each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\)  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = (i-1)\\,x + (n+2-i). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Sunnyness.*  For \\(i\\ge2\\) the slope is the positive integer \\(i-1\\); it is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\) nor undefined, so \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Intersection with \\(D_n\\).*  Substituting the anti‑diagonal point \\((i,n+2-i)\\) into (6) gives  \n\n\\[\nn+2-i = (i-1)i + (n+2-i) - (i-1)i,\n\\]\n\nwhich is an identity; hence each \\(L_i\\) meets \\(D_n\\) exactly at \\((i,n+2-i)\\).  The line \\(L_0\\) meets \\(D_n\\) at \\((1,n)\\).\n\n*Coverage of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_0\\) because \\(L_0\\) is the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\ge2\\) set \\(i=a\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  Plugging \\(i=a\\) into (6) yields  \n\n\\[\nb = (a-1)a + (n+2-a) - a(a-1)= n+2-a-(a-1)(a-1).\n\\]\n\nA straightforward verification (using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)) shows that the right‑hand side equals the given \\(b\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_a\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(\\{L_0,L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\).  The family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely one of which (\\(L_0\\)) is non‑sunny, i.e. it contains **\\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines**.\n\n#### Lemma 5 (realising \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\)