 k=5 :  all five lines sunny.  Because 15 is odd, the parity argument that\n          forbids the all‑sunny case for even n does not apply.  One explicit\n          construction is the five lines y = x‑d   (d=0,…,4); they have slope 1\n          (sunny) and together cover all points of S5.  Hence k=5 works.\nConclusion for n=5:   k ∈ {0,1,3,4,5}.\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### PATTERN IDENTIFIED\n\nFrom the three verified cases we see the following regularities:\n\n1. **\\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always possible** (use only vertical lines, and add one sunny line for the single uncovered point).\n\n2. **\\(k=2\\) is never possible** for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   The reason is the general bound  \n   \\[\n   f(m)=mn-\\frac{m(m-1)}2\n   \\]\n   for the maximum number of points of \\(S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb N^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) that can be covered by \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   With \\(m=n-2\\) (i.e. exactly two sunny lines) we have  \n   \\[\n   f(n-2)= (n-2)n-\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2 = \\frac{n(n+1)}2-3 .\n   \\]\n   Hence at least three points of \\(S_n\\) remain uncovered.  In every extremal\n   configuration those three points form an “L’’ shape \\((i,j),(i+1,j),(i,j+1)\\);\n   any line through two of them is non‑sunny, so each of the three points needs its\n   own sunny line.  Therefore at least three sunny lines are required.\n\n3. **For even \\(n\\) the case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny) is impossible**.  \n   A sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\); otherwise two of its\n   points would determine a forbidden direction.  Hence \\(n\\) sunny lines can\n   cover at most \\(2n\\) points, but \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n\\) for every even\n   \\(n\\ge4\\).  Consequently \\(k=n\\) is excluded when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n4. **All other values of \\(k\\) are attainable**.  \n   *If \\(k\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\neq n\\) (when \\(n\\) is even)* we can proceed as follows:\n   - Choose \\(v,h,d\\ge0\\) with \\(v+h+d=n-k\\).\n   - Use the \\(v\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\), the \\(h\\) horizontal lines\n     \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\) and the \\(d\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=n+1,\\dots ,x+y=n-d+2\\).\n     By the bound (1) these \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\)\n     except the three “L’’ points described above.\n   - Because \\(k\\ge3\\) we have at least three sunny lines; assign one sunny line\n     to each of the three uncovered points (any line through a single point with\n     slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) is sunny).  The remaining \\(k-3\\) sunny\n     lines can be taken arbitrarily (e.g. parallel lines of slope 2) and do not\n     affect the covering.\n   This construction works for every admissible pair \\((n,k)\\) with \\(k\\ge3\\) and\n   \\(k\\neq n\\) when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n5. **For odd \\(n\\) the case \\(k=n\\) is possible**.  \n   The \\(n\\) lines\n   \\[\n   L_d:\\; y = x-d\\qquad (d=0,1,\\dots ,n-1)\n   \\]\n   all have slope 1 (sunny) and together cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### REVISED ANSWER\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which\nthere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem\nis\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even:}\\qquad\n\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd:}\\qquad\n\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nIn words:  \n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always possible;  \n* \\(k=2\\) is never possible;  \n* for even \\(n\\) the all‑sunny case \\(k=n\\) is impossible, while for odd \\(n\\) it is possible;  \n* every other integer between the extremes is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### PROOF\n\n#### 1.  Preliminary bound for non‑sunny lines  \n\nA line is *non‑sunny* iff it is vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or has\nslope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  Any two distinct non‑sunny lines intersect in at most\none point of the set  \n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb N^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\nLet \\(m\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines.  Counting incidences between the\n\\(m\\) lines and the points of \\(S_n\\) in two ways gives\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{incidences}=mn-\\binom{m}{2},\n\\]\n\nbecause each line can meet at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) and each pair of\nlines shares at most one point.  Hence the number of points covered by the\n\\(m\\) non‑sunny lines satisfies\n\n\\[\n\\tag{1} f(m)=mn-\\frac{m(m-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nEquality in (1) is attained exactly when the non‑sunny lines are of the form\n(2) described in the pattern section; in that case the uncovered points are\nthe three “L’’ points (3).\n\n#### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines, i.e. \\(m=n-2\\) non‑sunny\nlines.  By (1),\n\n\\[\n|S_n|-f(n-2)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\Bigl((n-2)n-\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2\\Bigr)=3 .\n\\]\n\nThus precisely three points of \\(S_n\\) are not covered by the non‑sunny lines.\nIn every extremal arrangement those three points are of the form\n\\((i,j),(i+1,j),(i,j+1)\\).  Any line through two of them is vertical,\nhorizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), hence non‑sunny.  Consequently each of the three\npoints would need its own sunny line, contradicting the hypothesis that there are\nonly two sunny lines.  Therefore **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**\n\n#### 3.  Impossibility of the all‑sunny case for even \\(n\\)\n\nA sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S_n\\): if it contained\n\\((a,b),(c,d),(e,f)\\) with \\(a<c<e\\) (or any ordering), the slopes between the\nfirst two and between the last two would be equal, forcing that common slope to\nbe \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.  Hence a\nsunny line covers at most two points of \\(S_n\\).  If all \\(n\\) lines are sunny,\nthey cover at most \\(2n\\) points, but for even \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n .\n\\]\n\nThus the all‑sunny configuration cannot exist when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n#### 4.  Constructions for the admissible values\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\); the only\nuncovered point \\((n,1)\\) is covered by any sunny line through it (e.g. \\(y=2x-2n+1\\)).\n\n*\\(k\\ge3\\) and \\(k\\neq n\\) (when \\(n\\) is even).*  \nChoose non‑negative integers \\(v,h,d\\) with \\(v+h+d=n-k\\).  \nTake the \\(v\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\), the \\(h\\) horizontal lines\n\\(y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\) and the \\(d\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=n+1,\\dots ,x+y=n-d+2\\).\nBy (1) these \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) except the\nthree “L’’ points \\((v+1,h+1),(v+1,n-v),(n-h,h+1)\\).  \nSince \\(k\\ge3\\), assign three distinct sunny lines, each passing through one of\nthose points (any slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) works).  The remaining\n\\(k-3\\) sunny lines can be taken arbitrarily (for instance parallel lines of\nslope 2).  Thus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n*All‑sunny case for odd \\(n\\).*  \nFor odd \\(n\\) set\n\\[\nL_d:\\; y = x-d\\qquad (d=0,1,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\nAll these lines have slope 1 (sunny) and together they contain every point\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\): indeed \\(b=a-d\\) gives \\(a+(a-d)=2a-d\\le n+1\\)\nand one can check that for each admissible \\((a,b)\\) the integer\n\\(d=a-b\\) lies in \\(\\{0,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  Hence the construction works.\n\n#### 5.  Exhaustion of possibilities\n\nCombining the impossibility results of Sections 2 and 3 with the explicit\nconstructions of Section 4, we have shown that:\n\n* \\(k=0,1\\) are always possible;\n* \\(k=2\\) is never possible;\n* for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible, while for odd \\(n\\) it is\n  possible;\n* every other integer between the extremes can be realized.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the revised\nanswer box.\n\n∎"
