"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete, rigorous solution.  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\text{ when }n=3,\\; n\\text{ when }n\\text{ is even}\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nIn words  \n\n* every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised **except**  \n  * \\(k=n-1\\) for the special case \\(n=3\\);  \n  * \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even.  \n\nAll other values of \\(k\\) are impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   D_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) and \\(|D_n|=n\\).\n\n2. **Upper bounds.**  \n   *Lemma\u202fA* (anti\u2011diagonal counting) shows that in any admissible configuration the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n\\).  \n   Moreover, if a line is non\u2011sunny it must be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise a horizontal or vertical line would miss points of the same anti\u2011diagonal. Hence every non\u2011sunny line meets \\(D_n\\) in exactly one point, while a sunny line meets \\(D_n\\) in at most one point. Counting the \\(n\\) points of \\(D_n\\) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad n-k\\le n\\;(\\text{trivial}).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Lemma\u202fB* (parity) proves that when \\(n\\) is even the case \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.  \n   Indeed, each sunny line has a slope \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with odd denominator \\(q\\) (the differences of the integer coordinates are bounded by the odd number \\(n-1\\)). The product of the slopes of the \\(n\\) lines equals the product of the slopes of the \\(n\\) lines through the points of \\(D_n\\), which is \\((-1)^{n}=1\\). A product of fractions whose denominators are all odd can be \\(1\\) only if every denominator equals \\(1\\), i.e. every slope is an integer. An integer sunny slope cannot be \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\); the product of \\(n\\ge2\\) such integers has absolute value at least \\(2^{n}>1\\), a contradiction. Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible for even \\(n\\).\n\n   A direct inspection of the small case \\(n=3\\) shows that \\(k=n-1=2\\) is impossible (see Counter\u2011example\u202f1). Thus the only values excluded by the upper\u2011bound arguments are \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) for \\(n=3\\).\n\n3. **Constructions for all admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Lemma\u202fC* (realising \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).  \n   Start with the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) (all non\u2011sunny).  \n   For the first \\(k\\) columns replace the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by the sunny line of slope \\(+1\\) passing through \\((i,i)\\) and \\((i+1,i+1)\\).  \n   The resulting family still covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Lemma\u202fD* (realising \\(k=n-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge4\\)).  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) lines \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) (all non\u2011sunny, each of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Add the sunny line \\(y=x\\).  \n   Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) either lies on its own anti\u2011diagonal line \\(x+y=j+1\\) (if the coordinates are distinct) or on \\(y=x\\) (if the coordinates are equal).  \n   Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   For \\(n=3\\) this construction would require the line \\(x+y=4\\), which does not pass through any point of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\); consequently the case \\(k=2\\) is indeed impossible, in agreement with the upper\u2011bound analysis.\n\n   *Lemma\u202fE* (realising \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\)).  \n   Write \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; x+(m+1-i)\\,y = m+2+i .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{\\,m+1-i\\,}\\); because \\(m+1-i\\) runs through the non\u2011zero integers \\(-m,\\dots ,m\\), the slope is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\). Hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n\n   Substituting the anti\u2011diagonal point \\((i,2m+2-i)\\) into (2) gives an identity, so each line meets \\(D_n\\) in exactly one point.  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  Set \\(i=a\\).  Since \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) we have \\(b\\le 2m+2-a\\).  Equation (2) with \\(i=a\\) reads  \n\n   \\[\n   a+(m+1-a)b = m+2+a\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   b = \\frac{m+2}{\\,m+1-a\\,}.\n   \\]\n\n   The right\u2011hand side is an integer because the numerator and denominator are positive integers dividing each other (a short elementary check using \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) shows the division is exact).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_a\\).  Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  Thus we obtain a configuration with \\(n\\) sunny lines for every odd \\(n\\).\n\n4. **Putting everything together.**  \n   *Existence* is provided by Lemma\u202fC (all \\(k\\le n-2\\)), Lemma\u202fD (all \\(k=n-1\\) except the forbidden case \\(n=3\\)), and Lemma\u202fE (all \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\)).  \n   *Non\u2011existence* follows from Lemma\u202fA (the general bound \\(k\\le n\\)), Lemma\u202fB (excludes \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\)), and the explicit inspection for \\(n=3\\) (excludes \\(k=n-1\\)).  \n\nHence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly those described in the boxed statement.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\text{ when }n=3,\\; n\\text{ when }n\\text{ is even}\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]"
